    "stdout_excerpt": "COUNTERFACT_JSON={\"expected\": {\"invalid\": [], \"missing\": [], \"refusal_class\": \"TmxProviderError\", \"refusal_code\": \"TMX_BAD_ROW\", \"rejected\": [], \"status\": null}, \"observed\": {\"invalid\": [], \"missing\": [], \"refusal_class\": \"TmxProviderError\", \"refusal_code\": \"TMX_BAD_ROW\", \"rejected\": [], \"status\": null}, \"reason\": \"the search operation selects four columns and _TmxEquitySearchSourceRow declares all four without defaults, so removing one key from one row is the tamper that prices this mapper's own screen: it refuses TMX_BAD_ROW from _equity_search_rows while the second row still validates, so the refusal is about one cell. The cell chosen is symbol, and the same probe shows why that is a schema claim rather than a natural-key claim: setting symbol to null SERVES two rows (the published TmxEquitySearch contract leaves it optional), so this witness prices the reply's column set, not the addressability of a hit. A list-shape tamper was this entry's first draft and is withdrawn rather than kept as a second arm: client.list_rows already refuses a non-list payload with TMX_BAD_ROWS, so that tamper would prove the client layer's screen while being read as the mapper's; untampered control produced 2 row(s) through the same seam\"}\nVERDICT=PASS\n"
  },
